  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... It will be hot Fri, prior to scattered to locally \nnumerous showers/storms that will focus over the Piedmont and wrn \nSandhills Fri afternoon and night and redevelop over the srn half of \nNC (especially along and south of US Hwy 64) on a less hot but still \nhumid Sat. \n\nAn anomalously strong subtropical ridge will weaken while drifting \nfrom the TN and lwr MS Valleys swwd to the srn Plains through Sat, \nduring which time at least a couple of convectively-enhanced mid-\nlevel disturbances may develop and track through its nern periphery \nfrom the OH Valley to the srn Middle Atlantic and Carolinas.  \n\nAt the surface, a lead effective front and composite outflow \nstretching somewhat discontinuously from nrn VA wwd through srn and \ncntl OH, IN, and IL, will progress swd and across VA and perhaps nrn \nNC by 12Z Fri. This boundary may be indiscernible from a lee trough \nthat will extend across the Piedmont during the morning and \nsubsequently progress sewd and across cntl NC through the day and \nserve as the leading edge of both less hot temperatures and richer \nlow-level moisture that will displace a residually-dry/continental \npre-frontal airmass deposited by a preceding Canadian high and dry \nair ridge. A closely-spaced, trailing synoptic front and nrn bound \nof rich low-level moisture characterized by ~70 F surface dewpoints, \nnow stretching across the nrn Middle Atlantic, srn Lower MI, and nrn \nIL, will also progress swd and through VA during the day Fri and \ninto-through cntl NC Fri night through Sat. \n\nInstability and shower/storm chances will probably become maximized \nin the moist axis between these two boundaries, favored over the \nPiedmont and wrn Sandhills Fri afternoon-night. Isolated strong to \nlocally damaging downbursts will be possible where storms occur \nduring the afternoon-early evening, when steep low-level lapse rates \nwill favor multi-cell organization and preferential cell development \nalong shared and swd-moving outflow, amid weak nly to nnely mean \nwinds. Weak shear and mid-level lapse rates, above the steep low-\nlevel ones driven by strong insolation/diurnal heating, will \notherwise limit severe potential. \n\nRedevelopment or re-intensification of otherwise shallow convection \non Sat will then be favored over the srn half of NC (especially \nalong and south of US Hwy 64) on a less hot but still humid Sat. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Monday will feature another round of scattered to \nlocally numerous showers/storms.\n\nThe models indicate a weak frontal wave will develop across the OH \nValley and across and offshore the Middle Atlantic Sun-Sun night, \nwith the trailing cold front forecast to settle through cntl NC on \nMon. Hot and humid conditions, and moderate instability, will result \nahead of that cold front and focus for additional, scattered to \nlocally numerous convection over cntl NC on Mon. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Nely flow and cold air damming will probably yield \nmuch cooler and cloudier conditions, possibly accompanied by periods \nof mainly light rain, during the middle of next week.\n\nFollowing Monday's cold frontal passage, a strong (~1035 mb) surface \nhigh is forecast to progress across cntl and ern Canada, while \nridging swd across the Middle Atlantic/Carolinas. This high/ \nanticyclone will be maintained by confluent mid/upr-level flow \ndownstream of a Rex blocking configuration invof the upr Great Lakes \nand trailing a vigorous trough forecast to progress through Atlantic \nCanada. Nely low-level flow and cold air damming will probably \nresult over the srn Middle Atlantic/Carolinas and yield much cooler \nand cloudier conditions, possibly accompanied by periods of mainly \nlight rain, during the middle of next week.",
